Z||000012||artherd||08-24-2001||02:31 AM||||Hi Fierokid, I'm not sure.... but I *don't* think so.  Keep in mind I've never owned a non-88 V6.<P>But I belive the 88s are the only year v6 that will accept the 4-cylinder's lquid filled engine mount. (same part number for v6 and l4 in 1988, and l4 in 1987 and 88 at least, maybe more years for the 4-cyl.)<P>what I do NOT know is, can you take the bracket off an 88 v6 car, and bold it to your non-88 v6, and will it work?  your 87 4-cyl cradle will take said mount (infact you could re-use the one you have, though I recomend replacement)  but I don't know if everything would line up.<P>Try it and tell us?  :P)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000013||Indiana_resto_guy||08-24-2001||08:10 AM||||As I recall, I needed to put a liquid filled on an '86 coupe.<BR>Have you called a parts store to see if the listing for the 84 shows a liquid filled one?<BR>If you have a solid rubber mount now, you may want to stick with it.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Neal||08-24-2001||11:43 AM||||it sounds like you have a choice of using the liquid filled and drilling new holes at the engine bracket. OR using the solid rubber mount and drilling new holes at the cradle.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fierokid87||08-24-2001||12:31 PM||||Thats the thing though.  I dont have a choice I dont think cuzz my cradle was drilled for the 4 cyld liquid mount.  thats what came on my car.  And the holes for the bracket on the engine line up with the mount.  But I wonder is i need to drill new holes for the solid mount because the 87 V-6 did not have the liquid mount.<P>Scott||||reg||1
